"\nTARGET 42 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_02\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 4007e9c45068a893867c0449aa093ce395574a54 48a8c66c9e36efac87d851daf92beedb75b4294f04732612ecd9a07b6fe39dcc RANGES [[1, 41]] MASKED []\n1: # 2026\u002d09\u002d02 work log\n2: \n3: ## Device\u002dtesting round: copy, contrast, and an operation that never said how it went\n4: \n5: Three findings from the maintainer testing the 20260902 H700 image, all fixed\n6: and committed; none of them are in that image.\n7: \n8: ### Copy sweep\n9: \n10: Every list on the cloud screens was missing its serial comma, and \"states\"\n11: was doing duty for two different files. Fixed across ten strings, and written\n12: down as three conventions in `.claude/rules/es\u002dnative\u002dui.md` so it does not\n13: drift back:\n14: \n15: \u002d serial comma, always;\n16: \u002d a battery save is a **game save**, a machine snapshot is a **save state**,\n17:   and bare \"saves\" is only acceptable where nothing contrasts with it;\n18: \u002d **Wi\u002dFi** hyphenated in user\u002dvisible text, whatever the settings key is\n19:   called.\n20: \n21: The sweep was mechanical once the strings were extracted from the diff against\n22: the upstream merge base \u2014 that is the right way to find \"screens we created\"\n23: without grepping the whole app and grading upstream's copy.\n24: \n25: ### The progress card blended into the game art\n26: \n27: `AsyncNotificationComponent` drew itself at 200 of 255. Every other themed\n28: surface \u2014 menus, dialogs, `GuiInfoPopup` past its fade\u002din \u2014 draws at full\n29: opacity and lets the theme decide how solid to be, and the shipped theme's\n30: menu background is an opaque `0x111111`. So the card was the single exception,\n31: and at 200 roughly a fifth of the box art behind it came through: a progress\n32: line on a smear that changed with the artwork. Now 255, via a named constant,\n33: which is a standardisation rather than a new choice. `frame.png`'s corners\n34: fade to nothing, so it still reads as a card.\n35: \n36: Worth noting how the answer was found: not by picking a nicer number, but by\n37: asking what every other surface does. There was a standard; this component was\n38: outside it.\n39: \n40: ### \"I walked away and have no idea whether it worked\"\n\nTARGET 43 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_03\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 5b832ffe0995b8cbf0475854090870f52dbdcf12 6af8214b296209e0474c84bde0337f0959b0a921899b1252993e08e87a91a62d RANGES [[1, 41]] MASKED []\n1: \n2: ## A stamp is not a screen\n3: \n4: The progress card closes itself when its job ends. That is correct for a\n5: scrape and wrong for a 1.4 GiB restore, which is exactly the thing somebody\n6: walks away from \u2014 and the maintainer did, came back to a sleeping handheld,\n7: and had no way to tell whether it had worked. They had to ask.\n8: \n9: The uncomfortable part: I had already been told this. The last\u002drun stamps went\n10: in yesterday *because* of the same complaint, and I treated them as the answer.\n11: They are not. A row in a menu serves somebody who thinks to go and look; it\n12: does nothing for somebody standing in front of the device when the surface\n13: they were watching disappears. Necessary, not sufficient \u2014 and I recorded the\n14: necessary half as though it closed the issue.\n15: \n16: `GuiCloudTransfer` is the fix: backup and restore own the screen until\n17: dismissed. Live line, a bar only where a real percentage exists behind it,\n18: elapsed, then the outcome. Input refused while running (nothing to choose, and\n19: a stray press should not dismiss a page somebody is waiting on); any button\n20: after. Built on `GuiBackup`'s shape rather than a new one, because that is\n21: already this codebase's answer to a long job with its own page.\n22: \n23: The rule now has a fourth tier and a deciding column \u2014 **Ends** \u2014 because the\n24: distinction that matters is not size or position but whether the surface\n25: outlives the work. Duration picks between a card and a page.\n26: \n27: ### Two bugs the same session, both found by the maintainer\n28: \n29: **BACK performed the action.** `GuiSettings::close()` runs `save()` then the\n30: finalize hook, so a page using `onFinalize` as its \"proceed\" signal fires on\n31: any exit. Pressing BACK on the system chooser started the restore. The fix\n32: needed the proceed flag read *inside* finalize rather than after `close()`,\n33: because close deletes the page along with the button closure currently\n34: executing \u2014 blindspot 4, walked up to and avoided only because it is written\n35: down.\n36: \n37: **Content transfers never reported progress.** rclone writes periodic stats to\n38: its log destination; with `\u002d\u002dlog\u002dfile` set and no `\u002d\u002dprogress` there is nothing\n39: on stdout. The saves path has always passed `\u002d\u002dprogress`; the two content\n40: scripts never did, so every content transfer since they were written was\n\nTARGET 44 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_04\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY c8a5b7813ac07096b01e5036e76dc565c8bb9041 1dec087a0bc4d81ff8b2f1923c35dd74779e9ba64e74916b60d09482a2f14a57 RANGES [[307, 338]] MASKED []\n307: ## 23:40 UTC \u2014 Cable replaced, wired back, and the H700 checks the stash was waiting on\n308: \n309: **The cause was the cable.** The maintainer swapped it at 23:25 UTC. Kernel log:\n310: `Link is Up 10 Mbps Half Duplex` at 23:25:07, down, then `Link is Up 1000 Mbps\n311: Full Duplex` at 23:25:10; `carrier_changes` has sat at 24 since. DHCP gave the\n312: reserved .53; `ip route get 1.1.1.1` goes out `enp128s31f6`. D\u002dINFRA\u002d005.\n313: \n314: **Hub back on the wired address.** Pinned .53 in the mac mini's `known_hosts`\n315: by byte\u002dcomparing `ssh\u002dkeyscan` of .53 against serval's own\n316: `/etc/ssh/ssh_host_{ed25519,ecdsa,rsa}_key.pub` \u2014 the definitive source, since\n317: this session runs on serval. Two failed\u002dclosed attempts first: an empty operand\n318: in the compare, then macOS `ssh\u002dkeyscan` putting its `# host:22 SSH\u002d2.0\u2026`\n319: banners on stdout so they landed in the diff. Neither was loosened; both were\n320: read. Inventory `ansible_host` \u2192 .53, comment rewritten to the verified story,\n321: `ansible serval \u002dm ping` \u2192 pong. lorry `2a7ee94`; the repo is three commits\n322: ahead of `origin/main` (`2199a91`, `3a55949`, `2a7ee94`), unpushed.\n323: \n324: **H700 post\u002dreboot checks (stash step 3).** The device answered again once the\n325: LAN path changed. `BUILD_ID=3624e2e329\u2026` \u2014 the image built and hashed at 21:05\n326: is what is running; `/storage/.update/` is empty; up since ~22:26 UTC. The\n327: read\u002donly `cloud_content_restore \u002d\u002dmatch` preview printed\n328: `megadrive|remove|52|53193852` among nine `sync|0|0` rows \u2014 the expected line,\n329: with the byte count the stash note did not mention. Still the maintainer's,\n330: hands on the device: exit a game while the boot sync is running and confirm the\n331: card says SKIPPED.\n332: \n333: **On my own error.** The 22:45 entry stated a cause I had inferred from one\n334: word of `ip` output. The correction cost three register rows and two lorry\n335: commits. The rule already exists (*Verify the artifact, not the report*); what\n336: this adds is that the cheapest moment to apply it is before writing the word\n337: \"because\".\n\nTARGET 45 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_05\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY a22ae1ae4ec4577dae26ae338b5fddaee962d1c6 60f6943350e9b040494f5ea61f032caaf3db78fb98d4b4008abf8d19c25033d7 RANGES [[1072, 1109]] MASKED []\n1072: ## 20:30 UTC \u2014 Council round 2, and two things learned about running it\n1073: \n1074: The conflict\u002dresolution council recursed to r2 after a genuine 2\u002d2\u002d1 tie and\n1075: has now cleared r2 Steps 2 and 3; the vote is running. Substantively the round\n1076: was worth its cost: the deletion\u002dpropagation fork closed without the\n1077: orchestrator arbitrating, because an argument nobody had made in r1 \u2014 that\n1078: D\u002dCLOUD\u002d030's compaction cannot terminate unless something retires the cloud\n1079: copy \u2014 moved two seats off \"never propagate\". Concurrency settled on archiving\n1080: the preimage in both directions rather than a hand\u002dbuilt two\u002dphase commit.\n1081: \n1082: Two process learnings, both recorded where they will be read again.\n1083: \n1084: **A watcher must not be able to match itself.** Both background completion\n1085: watchers were `until [ \"$(pgrep \u002dfc council\u002dinvoke)\" = 0 ]` loops, and the\n1086: loop's own command line contains that string, so `pgrep` counted the watcher\n1087: and the condition could never be true. They spun for 1h40m and 1h26m; r2 Step\n1088: 3 had actually finished at 19:14 and nothing noticed until the maintainer\n1089: asked. The fix is a pattern the watcher's own command line cannot contain:\n1090: `pgrep \u002dfc 'council\u002dinvoke[.]ts \u002d\u002dmember'` \u2014 the bracket is literal in the\n1091: watcher and matches a dot in the target. Generalises past pgrep to every\n1092: self\u002dreferential filter (`ps | grep`, `docker ps \u002d\u002dfilter`, log tails greping\n1093: for a string the tailing command itself prints).\n1094: \n1095: **Seat output length is a property of the seat.** Maintainer: *\"Gemini and\n1096: Mistral will always come back with thinner plans.\"* Written up in\n1097: `.claude/rules/adversarial\u002dcouncil.md`; the point worth keeping is that the\n1098: orchestrator should stop reporting brevity as a finding, and should instead\n1099: watch the vote, since those two seats have taken zero votes across two rounds\n1100: while their arguments were adopted by name.\n1101: \n1102: Also this session: D\u002dCLOUD\u002d032 records the maintainer's restatement that\n1103: undoing a conflict decision is a first\u002dclass requirement and that edge cases\n1104: may inform but not drive the design; it is carried into the r2 vote prompt as\n1105: an amendment to the problem context, identical for all five seats. And #71\n1106: files a verified shipped defect the council found \u2014 a user\u002dedited `RCLONEOPTS`\n1107: that omits `\u002d\u002dfilter\u002dfrom` drops the saves allowlist entirely, leaving the\n1108: whole ROM library as the transfer root.\n\nTARGET 46 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_06\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 1e7db9567ba6cfbc4c3cb425c0be58aba40ad659 24f4b5fa035fb8b993d1fa86dad4c3fee6755aa3cc2c4cad636ce6d816161066 RANGES [[349, 365]] MASKED []\n349: ## 22:20 UTC \u2014 Gate 0: the harness ran for the first time (#35)\n350: \n351: `begin\u002ddelivery` on #11 through Step 4, with the maintainer's rule at\n352: confirmation \u2014 conflict and bisync testing on a VM pair only, never a\n353: handheld (D\u002dQA\u002d009) \u2014 and the addendum on #26 as the prior retro. Then\n354: task 1: `tools/vm\u002dpair` (two guests from one image, the QA key over\n355: serial in each); task 2: MinIO answers the guest, the allowlist behaves\n356: under the scratch root; task 3: the baseline \u2014 the harness's first\n357: execution ever, seventeen steps, eight checks failed; task 4: the repairs,\n358: all but one in the harness, the one in `backuptool`; task 5: `PASSED`,\n359: 48 checks, on WebDAV and on MinIO, from the guest, with a script fixed on\n360: the host carried by `\u002d\u002dpath\u002dprefix` rather than an image. Evidence on\n361: #35, boxes ticked on it, the old\u002dimage half of the key box split off and\n362: left open with the reason. Next, task 6: the fixtures, each a failing\n363: commit first.\n364: \n\nTARGET 47 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_07\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY a9e13391ebbe057a475e4a0393df6518bbf9346e 0798fedf0f3b3584a27939654833cb4933dba52052530b35ad9b258e6606c75a RANGES [[114, 136]] MASKED []\n114: ## 21:15 UTC \u2014 Three dead RetroAchievements switches (#80), and the day's build on both handhelds\n115: \n116: The maintainer noticed CHALLENGE INDICATORS did nothing. It never had:\n117: EmulationStation writes `retroachievements.challenge_indicators`, `.encore`\n118: and `.unofficial`, `setsettings.sh` read `.challengeindicators`, `.active`\n119: and `.testunofficial`, and the shipped `system.cfg` seeded two of the old\n120: names at 0 \u2014 an upstream bug, on every ROCKNIX device. Fixed by reading\n121: the name ES writes and falling back to the old one, in the script and in\n122: the Dolphin and melonDS achievement scripts, with the defaults re\u002dseeded;\n123: proven on the VM by running the shipped and fixed scripts over three config\n124: shapes (the commit carries the table). `pr/retroachievements\u002dkeys` is built\n125: by content against `upstream/next` and waits for the maintainer's word.\n126: The device had in fact been handing RetroArch `\"0\"` for the indicators, so\n127: what the maintainer saw bottom\u002dright is another widget \u2014 RetroArch's\n128: progress tracker is the candidate; asked.\n129: \n130: The H700 was built twice: the first from a worktree `fork\u002dworktree sync`\n131: had skipped because the image step rewrites a tracked doc (the tool now\n132: names the file; the rule now says check `git log \u002d1` against `next` before\n133: `make`). `5f00ab7c26` went onto the RG35XX SP and the RG SP, each transfer\n134: and each reboot asked for and answered (D\u002dQA\u002d011 came out of the first\n135: attempt, when two waiters would have staged on their own).\n\nTARGET 48 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_08\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 8150edd3401ad9fff6fdf46a9a10ab007749abfd 0138d8a22cc045a61ca16d3da94ac81599070775c90b16ff6d3e6ae02b68730b RANGES [[784, 793]] MASKED []\n784: ## 23:10 UTC \u2014 two upstream PRs opened on the maintainer's word\n785: \n786: ROCKNIX/distribution#3285 (`automount: wait for an expected external card\n787: before using internal`, #84) and #3286 (`rocknix: runemu.sh returns how the\n788: launch ended`, #90). Both by content from `upstream/next` `14af940019`, one\n789: commit each, no personal paths, fork references removed from comments\n790: (`cloud_capture`, issue numbers, register ids). The exit\u002dstatus PR carries\n791: both files because the trap fix alone would turn every hotkey exit into a\n792: \"failed launch\" in upstream EmulationStation.\n\nTARGET 49 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_09\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 06770172a0aae726848d3b2c9653e31692fb5290 cc8553647e8a998c3986307886e01ff1b5c1ef18d3dc4c34143112639d219594 RANGES [[197, 227]] MASKED []\n197: ## 22:50 UTC \u2014 The RG SP incident, what could still be read, and the maintainer's direction\n198: \n199: After `d574edf975` went to both handhelds the RG SP lost Wi\u002dFi about a minute\n200: into its first boot with the startup sync mid\u002dcompare (113/113), the card\n201: stayed put because the rclone runs carry no timeouts, the maintainer forced it\n202: off, and both `system.cfg` and `es_settings.cfg` came back as defaults \u2014 the\n203: two files a clean shutdown rewrites, truncated when power was cut a few seconds\n204: into that shutdown. The Wi\u002dFi module did not enumerate on the next boot and did\n205: on the one after. `/storage/.cache/cloud_sync` stamps survived and fixed the\n206: timeline: the boot capture pass finished at 21:34:09Z, the startup sync never\n207: stamped, no cloud file changed in the window \u2014 the link went first, the sync\n208: hung second, the freeze (whatever it was) third. Root cause of the drop:\n209: unknown; nothing in the build touches kernel or drivers; the RG35XX SP is fine.\n210: Everything that would have explained it lives in RAM on a handheld.\n211: \n212: Maintainer: *\"Did we discover a race condition error? Do we need to explore\n213: more what happens if Wi\u002dFi drops while certain actions are happening with\n214: Cloud Sync\u2026\"* \u2014 not a race, an unbounded failure path (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d075). Filed\n215: #101 (bounded syncs, cancel\u002don\u002dlaunch), #102 (incident), #103 (fault\u002dinjection\n216: matrix on the VM, bounded runs, post\u002dfailure probe \u2192 69, startup on a settled\n217: connection, interface\u002dthread audit), #104 (persistent journal/logs, watchdog +\n218: crash store). Settings restored from the morning archive with the Wi\u002dFi lines\n219: carried across (ES stopped first so its exit could not clobber the restored\n220: file); verified. Order: #103, then #104. Three implementers started at 22:47Z\n221: on `feature/link\u002dloss` (rocknix and ES).\n222: \n223: Lesson for the runbook: on a handheld that misbehaves, read `uptime`,\n224: `journalctl \u002dk`, and the `/storage/.cache/cloud_sync` stamps *before* anyone\n225: holds the power button \u2014 the power button is what destroyed the evidence and\n226: the settings here.\n"
